  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday) \nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe extended forecast continues to trend drier and cooler as the \nupper trough continues to lift northward, while a small disturbance \nbecomes near stationary over the Big Country region. Meanwhile to \nour west, we will see the upper level high center across the \nSWCONUS, which will help promote northerly flow aloft across the \nregion. Temperatures Thursday will vary, while also being dependent \non the amount of moisture still in place along with rainfall totals \nfrom Tuesday and Wednesday. Highs will likely range from the lower \n70s across northwest counties up to the 80s in southeastern \ncounties. A more noticable change in temperatures is expected \ntowards late week, as a FROPA swings through the area in response to \nlongwave troughing across the CONUS/Canadian border allows the front \nto surge southward late Thursday night. A period of gusty winds will \nbe possible behind this boundary, as we see sharp pressure gradient \nincreases, though this will be short-lived with afternoon \ntemperatures in the 60s and 70s both Friday and Saturday.",
